WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:05.400
When you're an adult, all you're learning
isn't always going to be inside a building.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.670
We don't really decide what happens every
day

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:11.200
unless there's a planned outing or event we're
going to attend.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:18.260
Most days we just kind of finish up where
we left up the day before on a project,

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:20.620
or sometimes we're just spontaneous.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:24.500
Anita Rios-Sherman has six children under
the age of 18,

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:27.340
five of them are on the autism spectrum.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:31.920
Today the four youngest children will spend
the morning at the local natural history museum

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.580
where they'll explore, read, draw and play
with each other.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:39.580
It's an educational philosophy known as 'unschooling'.

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:42.960
They decided to try unschooling when the local
public schools

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.660
failed to meet the needs of her oldest son.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:50.100
She found that he benefited from a totally
unstructured approach to learning

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.420
and then tried the same method with her other
children.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:56.860
There are many things about the unschooling
philosophy that work really well

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:00.550
with children that are on the autism spectrum.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:04.020
A lot of it has to do with allowing them to
explore their passions,

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:06.360
for them not have to earn that time to explore
those things

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:09.960
'cause passions can turn into real life careers.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.560
School is a place where children learn to
be stupid.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:18.100
And the process that makes them stupid, at least
stupid in school,

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.880
is other people trying to control their learning.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.180
That's John Holt, who's the intellectual godfather
of the modern home schooling movement.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:29.020
In a sense he's the moral leader of a movement
in this country

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.670
on the part of a significant number of American
families

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:34.800
to take their kids out of school and teach
them at home.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.520
There's a common perception that home schoolers
are social conservatives

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.420
who want to shield their children from the
modern world.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:45.020
And it's good for the child to get out of
the home, get away from mom and dad

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.900
and talk to other people because when they become adults, they've got that problem.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:52.060
But Holt had a different view of the dangers
of school.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:57.140
In his ten books, Holt argued that children
were capable of self directed learning,

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:01.780
possessing a natural curiosity that's quashed
by modern schools.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:04.240
They  treat them like an empty receptacle

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:09.200
into which they are gonna pour whatever learning
they think they ought to have.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:12.980
In unschooling we say, people have been 'oh
so you don't believe in schools?'

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.960
It's tricky because what John means by unschooling

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:22.540
is learning that doesn't have to take place
at home and that doesn't look like school,

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:23.760
you know?

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.420
Because we're learning all the time.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:30.480
Pat Farenga is the publisher of a magazine
turned website that promotes Holt's philosophy

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.380
and the unschooling movement.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:38.520
Schools believe, and most teachers believe
childrenw ill not learn unless they are taught.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:43.220
And in order to be taught the teacher must
have x number of years of training

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:46.480
and they can only teach the subjects they've
been trained in.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:52.500
However, children and most adults when they're
not in school and most kids when they're not in school,

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:58.220
learn holistically, they learn from all their
senses and they learn from their experiences,

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.380
they're relationships

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:08.900
and to just bottle it up and say that now
we're gonna learn math is artificial.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:12.820
We were the only game in town in the 1980s
and 90s.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:15.720
Unschooling was pretty much a small niche.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:20.980
Now though, you go online there's anything
from Pagan unschoolers to radical unschoolers

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:24.840
and that to me is a great sign because there's
a lot more of us!

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:30.380
But while unschooling is closely tied to home
schooling, the two are not synonymous.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:36.460
Farenga maintains a list of alternative schools
that embrace some form of self directed learning.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:41.580
I absolutely consider unschooling a spectrum,
and that's, and again if you read John's books,

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:45.700
Freedom and Beyond, and Instead of Education
in particular,

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:50.860
he talks about other places and, you know,
where children and adults can be to learn

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:51.980
besides school.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:57.660
One such example is the Houston Sudbury School,

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:03.060
based on the Sudbury Valley school model pioneered
in Massachusetts in 1968.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:08.040
Students here who range in age from six to
seventeen play and learn with each other,

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.040
attend optional lessons in various subjects,

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:15.460
and each have an equal vote on how the school
is run and money allocated.

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:18.470
I had been unschooling my daughter since she
was born

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:22.020
but had encountered some limitations to that,

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:27.540
primarily how much I as a parent had to be
involved and decision making.

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:30.040
So I became interested in this idea of

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:34.760
'was there a school that carried the same
philosophy as unschooling

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:38.620
but gave kids some autonomy from their parents?'

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:40.420
And I had heard about Sudbury,

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:45.200
so the founders really came from the unschooling
community here in Houston.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:48.960
So in this environment the kids really can
use the resources here,

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:55.040
the space, the community, the money and really
create the life that they want.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:00.880
You don't have to like, sit in one space like
you just get to go wherever, I mean there's rules,

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:02.940
but you can just do whatever you want.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:06.240
So for a long time in school I had a lot of
problems with learning

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:09.200
and I was in a lot of special ed classes.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:14.080
The unstructured environment has been probably
one of the best things to happen to me in my life

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:19.580
to not be going to school all day and just
be not happy with life.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:22.860
You're not being in a classroom for days

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:26.710
with your mental health slowly deteriorating
because of endless class work

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:31.660
you're going to be interacting with people
and developing more social skills and responsibility

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:34.440
because everybody is held accountable for
what they do here.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:38.580
So in Sudbury you get a chance to really explore
what you want to do

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:43.980
instead of just being forced to do stuff that
really doesn't interest you.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:46.140
There is just so much freedom.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:53.580
In public school you kinda just like sit at
a desk and listen to your teacher and that's it.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:56.250
I definitely learned what public school wanted
me to learn.

00:05:56.250 --> 00:06:00.430
I listened in class, I was, like, payed attention,
I got good grades

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:04.300
but here I learn but here I learn about what
I want to learn about.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:08.460
They even have a daily judicial conference
where they deal with rule breakers.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:14.060
During today's conference they sent a six
year old out to investigate who crumpled up a print out-

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:15.880
Do you know who crumpled it up?

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:19.240
-but came up empty handed and decided to drop
the matter.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:24.120
While in a public school you'd have to go
to a teacher or talk about something that happened

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:26.800
and typically they don't do a good job of
sorting out a lot of those things.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:32.600
In JC if something happens, even something
small, you write that up and it gets talked about.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:35.740
It's a very orderly thing, there's no arguments,

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:40.180
it's mostly about figuring out what happened
and what can be done better.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:43.980
Enrolling in a school like Sudbury can be
really scary,

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:47.820
they're not getting what they'd be getting
in a traditional education.

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:53.840
I was really afraid in thinking about this
completely unstructured living for kids.

00:06:53.840 --> 00:07:01.120
But in my experience these kids actually end
up quite a bit more prepared, in some ways,

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:03.340
than someone who has had a traditional education

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:08.620
because they're actually participating in
a lot more of the real life activities

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:10.040
that people do.

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:15.760
Making a huge decision for a group, with the
members of a group,

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:17.580
when the group doesn't always agree.

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:22.620
While some parents might cringe to see their
kids spending much of the day just hanging out,

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:25.620
playing video games or looking at their phones,

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:30.240
believers in the Sudbury approach maintain
that the kids will learn what they need

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:31.700
when they need to do it.

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:38.140
I consider it a huge disadvantage to keep
kids from these devices and this technology

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:44.060
which is what they need to be learning how
to navigate in our culture.

00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:48.320
Many of the kids who are interested in gaming
and computers

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:55.060
are the kids who will grow up to be in those
fields making the next big evolution in technology.

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:59.860
There's plenty of time to gain everything
that you can gain at public school

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:01.000
right here at Sudbury

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:04.720
and a lot of students do and they actually
learn it

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:08.080
as opposed to pretending to learn it for a
test.

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:11.680
If the whole high school happens in six months
at a Sudbury school

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:16.500
I still believe they learn it better than
they would over years and years

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:18.980
of being forcedWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWW to memorize it.

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:25.220
Like most Sudbury schools, this one is private
with a 7 thousand dollar annual tuition fee

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:29.000
that they often modify to accommodate lower
income parents.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:34.500
We would love for the school to be available
to families regardless of their income

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:41.000
but I would not want to enter into any kind
of situation where we are accountable to the state.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:42.300
What do we want?

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:43.720
School choice!

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:44.980
When do we want it?

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:46.320
Now!

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:47.880
While the school choice movement

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:52.520
has brought explosive growth in the number of charter schools nationwide,

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:55.080
unschoolers haven't felt much effect.

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:57.920
Homeschoolers haven't been able to take advantage
of school choice

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:02.660
because there's no funding available for home
schooling, that's not considered a choice.

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:06.280
That's changed somewhat with educational savings
accounts

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:11.800
which allow homeschoolers in some states to
set aside a portion of their tax dollars for education.

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:14.880
But Farenga is skeptical of government involvement.

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:17.980
When the school superintendent in the district
where he lives

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:20.940
earmarked funds for homeschoolers like him
in the 90s,

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:26.100
he eventually rescinded the money on the grounds
that their activities were noneducational.

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:32.020
99% of the stuff we did in this house I'd
say, with the board games behind me and projects,

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:35.300
there's a lot of different things that are
educational.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:40.680
If you believe that you're learning all the
time, and we are learning all the time,

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:42.140
the world is your oyster.

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:45.460
But instead it's like 'oh no, that's not educational.'

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:46.820
Well who determines that?

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:51.620
Instead Farenga continues to advocate for
unschooling as a social movement

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:56.440
focused on the rights and personal freedoms
of children to find their own way.

00:09:56.450 --> 00:10:01.140
Right now, the best thing you can do if you
can't home school your child

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:04.740
is ignore as much of school as possible

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:07.440
and pay attention to what your child really wants to do

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:09.000
and work with that.

